#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491265} :activity/id #uuid "5396071c-65c8-5ca3-8777-9084be368206" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/id #uuid "ffe9e721-b26e-55d0-9e1f-e1d2a56a5a72" :topic/math-academy-id 3179} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a488a6a-c562-5116-a808-9606ad5fcd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241996} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9114ac-eb6e-5022-a9dc-0dfb399af7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242175} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe35dec-6d42-59f5-8111-b8e850cce8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242858} {:answer/id #uuid "cb622b0b-0e9a-5e4a-8f28-6fb1f1e8c0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242971} {:answer/id #uuid "e106123d-d9ac-599d-b55f-445e9e4d1b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180} :answer-field/id #uuid "579ac401-6a63-5a61-82b5-87d55aa593a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe2488e6-1056-5c66-a150-25aaef8df7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108858" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3aab70-1877-5ee8-a896-a828fe6ad391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241454} {:answer/id #uuid "61f69b05-6c80-5dbd-8fb8-f0aead30c675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+y+2)^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242059} {:answer/id #uuid "68e92ed8-70bc-5a3e-a2e3-29f9b53dd4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242122} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3ea3a5-0dd3-5cea-8cf5-a5dedd54b5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242544} {:answer/id #uuid "e050865e-0cfc-5a7c-8d03-dafcfb932d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242544} :answer-field/id #uuid "077176d7-a950-5c43-bdf4-07d7b8ff6ea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c93f6fd2-747e-585d-8694-c18e2cf8aef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108840" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y+2)^{3}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+2)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2(z+2)^{2}} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{2}} & =C-2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}=C-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490570} {:answer/id #uuid "783a347a-bf54-5822-92c5-cd38e0951ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x-y+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490749} {:answer/id #uuid "7a868760-39cb-55eb-9223-ed909c3d82ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490754} {:answer/id #uuid "d3724410-1b69-54fd-82ce-033b21b6c201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490907} {:answer/id #uuid "ecddebaf-9db9-54b0-8d19-3046d28f4b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490570} :answer-field/id #uuid "42278d90-82a6-5f26-9592-9f5a4df7efef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f664cca855c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-149719" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x-y+2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =z+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=z.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|.$"} :step/id #uuid "a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e859479-1214-5aa1-9663-6484c58e67d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378410} {:answer/id #uuid "461504be-45af-5511-a6e4-22297a20ac20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378411} {:answer/id #uuid "fe707789-7e96-5e0d-b56c-0e2e3ceb68e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378412} {:answer/id #uuid "9c36d855-eba9-57f3-8ba4-8ac6197d9a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378413} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1f358a-a2f0-5f9b-b0e9-1d77c448acf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378412} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff18d72-ae56-5bb5-b84e-265967baa590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f97d6af-c63f-5608-867b-bbac32e035d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108807" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-8x).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3y-8x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3y-8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{8}{3} & =3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$"} :step/id #uuid "c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdf75de-061d-588c-a360-fed5ae1090a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272935} {:answer/id #uuid "532850a1-3a73-586a-8762-10e055fa15e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{z-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273094} {:answer/id #uuid "6375c28c-5e9e-5f53-8864-e51769fb068f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273243} {:answer/id #uuid "c7aff33e-bce5-51d7-9451-a5e068d2a70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2z-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274124} {:answer/id #uuid "e2993fb8-03c7-5b2a-9fb0-686528082cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7af1607-0564-589d-85f1-119c58380d52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dabceef6-e8d3-5182-b240-ffcea0ff3af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149710" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{x-2y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt[3]{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491256}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490502}]}
